=== Paul's Lament for the Jews
--- Paul's Heart for Unbelieving Israel [title]
overline: Romans 9–11
heading: Paul's Heart for Unbelieving Israel
sub: Paul's Deep Concern for Unbelieving Israel, His Kinsmen After the Flesh
refs: Romans 9:1-5; Romans 10:1-4; Romans 11:1-7

--- Romans 9:1-5
kicker: Paul's Lament
scripture: Romans 9:1-5 | mark: great heaviness and continual sorrow in my heart; I could wish that myself were accursed from Christ for my brethren, my kinsmen according to the flesh

--- Romans 10:1-4
kicker: Paul's Lament
scripture: Romans 10:1-4 | mark: they have a zeal of God, but not according to knowledge

--- Romans 11:1-7
kicker: Paul's Lament
scripture: Romans 11:1-7 | mark: God forbid; a remnant according to the election of grace; the election hath obtained it, and the rest were blinded

--- Burden and remnant
panel: Paul's Burden | Deep love and sorrow for his unbelieving kinsmen after the flesh, yet they were not saved because of unbelief and pursuit of righteousness by works.
panel: The Righteous Remnant | Believing Jews (like Paul) obtained righteousness by faith and grace, showing God has not cast away His people, because a remnant are preserved by grace through faith.

--- But the rest?
p: But the rest?
scripture: Romans 11:15

=== One People of God
--- One People of God [title]
overline: Romans 11
heading: One People of God
sub: From Adam to Christ • The Called-Out by Faith

--- Beginning with Seth
kicker: Calling on the Name of the Lord
scripture: Genesis 4:26 | mark: then began men to call upon the name of the LORD
scripture: Romans 10:13 | mark: For whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved
p: The line of faith, those who call on the Lord, begins early, with Seth’s generation, not limited to physical descent from Abraham.

--- The Sons of God
kicker: Believers in the Coming Redeemer
scripture: Romans 8:14 | mark: For as many as are led by the Spirit of God, they are the sons of God
p: The “sons of God” throughout Scripture have always been believers looking to the Redeemer who liveth, from Adam’s faithful line through the patriarchs, prophets, and now all who are in Christ by faith.

--- The inward Jew
kicker: Circumcision of the heart
scripture: Romans 2:28-29 | mark: But he is a Jew, which is one inwardly; and circumcision is that of the heart, in the spirit, and not in the letter

--- Not all Israel
kicker: Not All Israel Which Are of Israel
scripture: Romans 9:6-8 | mark: For they are not all Israel, which are of Israel; They which are the children of the flesh, these are not the children of God: but the children of the promise are counted for the seed
scripture: Galatians 4:28 | mark: Now we, brethren, as Isaac was, are the children of promise

--- Gentiles once strangers
kicker: Now Fellowcitizens
scripture: Ephesians 2:11-13 | mark: without Christ, being aliens from the commonwealth of Israel, and strangers from the covenants of promise, having no hope, and without God in the world; But now in Christ Jesus ye who sometimes were far off are made nigh by the blood of Christ
scripture: Ephesians 2:19 | mark: ye are no more strangers and foreigners, but fellowcitizens with the saints, and of the household of God

--- One in Christ
kicker: No Distinction
scripture: Galatians 3:26-29 | mark: For ye are all the children of God by faith in Christ Jesus; There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither bond nor free, there is neither male nor female: for ye are all one in Christ Jesus

--- The Israel of God
kicker: All Israel Shall Be Saved
scripture: Romans 11:26 | mark: all Israel shall be saved
scripture: Galatians 6:16 | mark: And as many as walk according to this rule, peace be on them, and mercy, and upon the Israel of God

--- The new and better covenant
scripture: Hebrews 8:13 | mark: A new covenant, he hath made the first old
scripture: Hebrews 8:6 | mark: a more excellent ministry, by how much also he is the mediator of a better covenant, which was established upon better promises

--- One called-out assembly
point: One assembly | Scripture reveals one continuous called-out assembly, the sons of God: all believers in the Redeemer which liveth, from Adam and Seth’s line calling on the Lord, through Abraham, Isaac, and the remnant, to all who are in Christ.
point: One body | Gentiles, once aliens and strangers, are now fellowcitizens with the saints, made one body, the true Israel of God by faith.
point: One change | The only change: the Old Covenant (with its shadows and types) is done away, fulfilled and replaced by the New and better Covenant with better promises in Christ.
point: No separate peoples | Jew and Gentile together form the Israel of God, defined by faith from beginning to end.

=== Dispensation in the Bible
--- Dispensation [title]
overline: Romans 11
heading: Dispensation
sub: Biblical Stewardship • Household Management of the Gospel

--- The Greek word Oikonomia
lead: The word "Dispensation" in the Greek, **Oikonomia** literally comes from οἶκος (oikos = "house") + νόμος (nomos = "law" or "management/rule"), so it fundamentally means **"household management," "administration," or "stewardship."**
p: A steward or house manager is entrusted to oversee, protect, and faithfully distribute what belongs to the master. In this case, Paul compares his role to being entrusted with the gospel to faithfully preach and deliver it to others.

--- A dispensation of the gospel
kicker: 1 Corinthians 9:17
scripture: 1 Corinthians 9:17 | mark: dispensation of the gospel

--- The dispensation of the grace of God
kicker: Ephesians 3:2
scripture: Ephesians 3:2 | mark: dispensation of the grace of God

--- The dispensation of God
kicker: Colossians 1:25
scripture: Colossians 1:25 | mark: dispensation of God

--- The fulness of times
kicker: Ephesians 1:10
scripture: Ephesians 1:10 | mark: dispensation of the fulness of times

--- Paul's use of the term
p: In the three passages where Paul applies "dispensation" to himself (1 Cor 9:17, Eph 3:2, Col 1:25), he is saying he has been entrusted/stewarded with the gospel and the grace of God to faithfully preach and administer it to others.
p: In Ephesians 1:10, the term refers to God's own stewardship/dispensation over the entire fullness of times, gathering all things together in Christ at the end.

--- One continuous gospel
lead: We all have a **dispensation of the gospel**, to keep the faith.
p: One continuous gospel in Christ from Adam through today.
scripture: Hebrews 1:1-2
p: The difference over time was revelation of mysteries, given by the word of God through the prophets, the preaching of Christ, and the inspired New Testament authors, which gives us full revelation.

--- No "Church age"
p: The Old Testament replaced by the New, a physical nation with a spiritual one.
p: The Bible does not say anywhere that there is a "Church age," or various "church ages" (Revelation 1-3).
p: The "Church" is the called-out assembly of believers at the resurrection or individual independent congregations gathering at their local NT church.

=== A Spectrum of Beliefs
--- A Theological Spectrum [title]
overline: Comparative theologies
heading: A Theological Spectrum
sub: Dispensationalism, Replacement and Covenant Theology, and other viewpoints in light of the Bible

--- Introduction
quote: I have no problem with the word 'dispensation.' It is a Bible word, but I do have a problem with dispensationalism which says that salvation comes in different ways in different dispensations. | Bro. Jack Hyles
p: We'll start with views many of us have heard, move through the spectrum, hit the biblical center, then see how far some drift into error.
- Mild Dispensationalism
- Traditional Dispensationalism / Zionism
- Replacement Theology
- Biblical Fundamentalism
- Supersessionism
- Mid-Acts Dispensationalism
- Hyper-Dispensationalism

--- Who holds which view
kicker: The spectrum
table: View | Who generally holds it
tr: Mild Dispensationalism | Most Independent Fundamental Baptists (IFB), many conservative evangelicals, Bible churches, teachers like Jack Hyles, John R. Rice.
tr: Traditional Dispensationalism / Zionism | Traditional dispensationalists, many Southern Baptists, Calvary Chapel, popular prophecy teachers (e.g., Tim LaHaye, John Walvoord, Hal Lindsey), and organizations like those associated with Scofield Bible notes.
tr: Replacement Theology | Many Independent Fundamental Baptists (especially NIFB and some who embrace the "replacement" label), conservative evangelicals who reject Zionism, post-trib/pre-wrath teachers, and others who emphasize the NT's fulfillment language while rejecting dispensational future-national-Israel programs.
tr: Biblical Fundamentalism | Bible-believing Christians rejecting covenant over-spiritualization and dispensational insertions, prioritizing plain text (many IFB, independent Baptists).
tr: Supersessionism | Roman Catholic Church, Eastern Orthodox, many mainline Protestants (Methodist, Episcopal), some early Church Fathers.
tr: Mid-Acts Dispensationalism | Mid-Acts "grace movement," Berean Bible Society, teachers like Cornelius Stam, Charles Baker. Less common in mainstream IFB.
tr: Hyper-Dispensationalism | Extreme KJV-only/hyper groups, Ruckman followers, Gene Kim/Bibleline Ministries, fringe online "grace" circles. Rejected by mainstream IFB/evangelicals.

--- Mild Dispensationalism [title]
overline: View 1
heading: Mild Dispensationalism

--- Mild Dispensationalism: what it teaches
kicker: Mild Dispensationalism
panel: Teaches | Dispensations as stewardships or "administrations" (from the Greek oikonomia), different ways God interacts with and tests humanity in various eras of history. **Salvation is always by grace through faith alone in the promised Christ** ([[Ephesians 2:8-9]]), looking forward to the cross (though some teach the understanding of a Messiah wasn't always clear, "through a glass darkly").
panel: Common eras taught | Innocence (Garden of Eden), Conscience (after fall to flood), Human Government (Noah onward), Promise (Abraham to Moses), Law (Moses to Christ), Grace/Church Age (Pentecost to rapture), Kingdom/Millennium (future 1,000-year reign).
panel: End times | Emphasizes end-times prophecy and charts laying out the timing of events according to their theology. Holds to a future 7-year Tribulation, pre-tribulation rapture, and eventual Millennial kingdom; rejects post-millennial, amillennial, and preterist views. Sees future restoration of the physical nation of Israel during the Tribulation, where many (if not all) Jews will see the signs and wonders and turn to Christ. Uses [[Romans 11:26]] as support.

--- Mild Dispensationalism: strength and issue
kicker: Mild Dispensationalism
panel: Who generally holds this view | Most Independent Fundamental Baptists (IFB), many conservative evangelicals, Bible churches, teachers like Jack Hyles, John R. Rice.
panel: Strength | Strongly affirms one way of salvation, grace through faith in Christ, in every era, whether OT saints looking forward to the promised Savior or NT believers looking back to the cross. Provides room for disagreement on exact timing of end-times events (e.g., pre-, mid-, or post-tribulation rapture details) to avoid division among believers, and acts as a practical compromise with the prevailing theology in modern evangelical circles.
panel-warn: Issue | Uses "dispensation" to refer to distinct ages/eras with different tests/rules of responsibility, which the Bible does not explicitly define this way. May unintentionally suggest that the "church" is separate from Israel, with corporate Israel receiving promises and inheritance that are promised to all believers as a whole (see [[Galatians 3:28-29]]; [[Ephesians 2:14-19]], one body, one faith, no distinction in Christ).

--- Traditional Dispensationalism / Zionism [title]
overline: View 2
heading: Traditional Dispensationalism / Zionism

--- Traditional Dispensationalism: what it teaches
kicker: Traditional Dispensationalism / Zionism
panel: Teaches | God works in distinct dispensations/ages with different tests/rules of responsibility (e.g., innocence, law, grace). The Church is seen as a "parenthesis" in God's primary plan for Israel, an ultimate failure or incomplete entity necessitating the future restoration of physical/national Israel. Uses the 7 churches in Revelation 1–3 as prophetic of distinct "church ages" (e.g., Ephesus = apostolic, Laodicea = apostate modern Church).
panel: Teaches, continued | Holds to a future literal 7-year Tribulation, pre-tribulation rapture of the Church, second coming, and restored national Israel in a literal millennial kingdom (with temple sacrifices as memorials). Ethnic Israel is still God's chosen people today, despite apostasy, with unconditional future promises to the nation as a whole. Pays lip-service to salvation always being by grace through faith, but often drifts into implying the OT dispensation required obedience to the law/works for blessing or acceptance.

--- Traditional Dispensationalism: strength and issue
kicker: Traditional Dispensationalism / Zionism
panel: Who generally holds this view | Traditional dispensationalists, many Southern Baptists, Calvary Chapel, popular prophecy teachers (e.g., Tim LaHaye, John Walvoord, Hal Lindsey), and organizations like those associated with Scofield Bible notes.
panel: Strength | Takes OT prophecies literally and emphasizes God's faithfulness to His promises. Strongly promotes evangelism and end-times awareness.
panel-warn: Issue | Pays lip-service to salvation always being by grace through faith, but often drifts into implying the OT dispensation required works of the law for acceptance/blessing. Treats the "Church" as an ultimate failure necessitating the restoration of physical Israel. Uses the 7 churches in Revelation as distinct ages of the "Church" (not stated in text). Sees ethnic Israel currently as still God's chosen people despite apostasy, potentially downplaying the NT emphasis on faith alone and one body in Christ ([[Galatians 3:28-29]]; [[Ephesians 2:14-19]]).

--- Replacement Theology [title]
overline: View 3
heading: Replacement Theology

--- Replacement Theology: what it teaches
kicker: Replacement Theology
panel: Teaches | The Church directly replaces Israel because of Israel's rejection of Christ. Israel has forfeited its promises and role as God's chosen nation; the Church inherits them spiritually. The New Covenant replaces the Old ([[Hebrews 8:13]]). OT promises (land, kingdom, blessings) are transferred to or fulfilled in the Church. No future distinct restoration or role for ethnic/national Israel as a nation. Any salvation for Jews is individual entry into the Church. Strongly affirms the biblical pattern of God choosing the lesser over the greater, the younger over the older, the new over the old (e.g., Seth over Cain, Isaac over Ishmael, Jacob over Esau, Judah over Reuben, David over Saul, Esther over Vashti, publicans/harlots over Pharisees, etc.).
panel: Note on Historic Premillennialism | Some who hold a similar view of replacement/fulfillment and a future literal kingdom for all believers (Jew and Gentile together) call it "historic premillennialism," future millennium after Christ's return, no pre-trib rapture, no separate Israel program.

--- Replacement Theology: strength and issue
kicker: Replacement Theology
panel: Who generally holds this view | Many Independent Fundamental Baptists (especially NIFB and some who embrace the "replacement" label), conservative evangelicals who reject Zionism, post-trib/pre-wrath teachers, and others who emphasize the NT's fulfillment language while rejecting dispensational future-national-Israel programs.
panel: Strength | Faithfully highlights the biblical pattern of replacement/fulfillment (lesser over greater, new over old) seen throughout Scripture. Rejects Zionism and any separate future program for national Israel. Holds to post-trib/pre-wrath timing and a millennial kingdom for all believers (Jew and Gentile together). Emphasizes faith in Christ alone and warns against relying on physical descent or works.
panel-warn: Issue | Can create too sharp a break with OT believers (while still acknowledging them as part of the Church), and in defending against critics sometimes over-emphasizes discontinuity and misses the full continuity of one vine/assembly by faith from Adam to now ([[Romans 11:16-24]]; [[Hebrews 11:39-40]]).

--- Biblical Fundamentalism [title]
overline: View 4
heading: Biblical Fundamentalism

--- Biblical Fundamentalism: what it teaches
kicker: Biblical Fundamentalism
panel: Teaches | One continuous vine/root in Christ ([[Romans 11:16-24]]): natural (Jewish) and wild (Gentile) branches one congregation/assembly in resurrection. All saved by calling on the Lord ([[Romans 10:13]]; [[Genesis 4:26]]), knowing Redeemer liveth ([[Job 19:25]]), esteeming Christ's riches. Gospel preached, witnessed by Moses/prophets ([[Hebrews 4:2]]; [[1 Peter 1:10-12]]), testified by apostles ([[1 Timothy 2:6]]), unbroken to believers today. Various covenants/revelations, but salvation always grace through faith, no different gospels. New Covenant ends Old ([[Hebrews 8:13]]); physical nation to spiritual ([[1 Peter 2:9]]). No separate peoples/programs: unbelief cuts off, faith grafts in, mercy to whosoever believes.
panel: Key Scriptures | Rom 11:16–24 (one olive tree); Gal 3:28–29 (one in Christ); Eph 4:4–6 (one body); Heb 11:39–40 (OT perfected with us); Rev 7:9–17 (all nations redeemed).

--- Biblical Fundamentalism: strength and issue
kicker: Biblical Fundamentalism
panel: Who generally holds this view | Bible-believing Christians rejecting covenant over-spiritualization and dispensational insertions, prioritizing plain text (many IFB, independent Baptists).
panel: Strength | Lets Scripture speak plainly, one gospel by grace through faith from Genesis to Revelation, one people (Israel of God by faith), no inserted programs or multiple gospels.
panel: Issue | None, aligns with NT teaching and avoids extremes.
refs: Romans 11:16-24; Galatians 3:28-29; Ephesians 4:4-6; Hebrews 11:39-40; Revelation 7:9-17

--- Supersessionism [title]
overline: View 5
heading: Supersessionism

--- Supersessionism
kicker: Supersessionism
panel: Teaches | Church supersedes/replaces Israel in God's plan due to unbelief. OT promises fulfilled spiritually in Church. No distinct future for ethnic Israel; salvation for Jews is individual conversion into Church. Often amillennial; no literal future kingdom.
panel: Who generally holds this view | Roman Catholic Church, Eastern Orthodox, many mainline Protestants (Methodist, Episcopal), some early Church Fathers.
panel: Strength | Affirms fulfillment in Christ and unity by faith.
panel-warn: Issue | May imply God abandoned Israel (contrary to [[Romans 11:1-2]]). Over-spiritualizes NT eschatology, potentially downplaying literal future resurrection/reign for all believers ([[Revelation 20:4-6]]).

--- Mid-Acts Dispensationalism [title]
overline: View 6
heading: Mid-Acts Dispensationalism

--- Mid-Acts Dispensationalism
kicker: Mid-Acts Dispensationalism
panel: Teaches | Church begins mid-Acts (Acts 9/13) with Paul, not Pentecost. Early Acts under "Kingdom gospel" for Jews (baptism, signs). Paul's gospel distinct for Gentiles. Twists "rightly dividing" ([[2 Timothy 2:15]]) to claim two gospels. Adds works subtly to both. False notion Paul's gospel different from Abraham's ([[Galatians 3:8]]), Jesus'/Apostles' ([[Acts 10:43]]; [[Acts 13:38-39]]). Rejects baptism/Lord's Supper for Church today.
panel: Who generally holds this view | Mid-Acts "grace movement," Berean Bible Society, teachers like Cornelius Stam, Charles Baker. Less common in mainstream IFB.
panel: Strength | Emphasizes grace alone and Paul's apostleship.
panel-warn: Issue | Multiple gospels accursed ([[Galatians 1:8-9]]). Adds works subtly. Creates unbiblical divisions, diminishing unity from Pentecost ([[Ephesians 4:4-6]]).

--- Hyper-Dispensationalism [title]
overline: View 7
heading: Hyper-Dispensationalism

--- Hyper-Dispensationalism
kicker: Hyper-Dispensationalism
panel: Teaches | Extreme Mid-Acts: Church begins post-Acts 28 or Paul's prison epistles. Rejects most NT for Church (Gospels, early Acts for Israel). Multiple gospels (Peter's, Paul's, others). Rejects baptism/Lord's Supper today. Some claim KJV advanced revelation corrects originals (Ruckman). Outlandish claims (millennium for physical Jews only, temple sacrifices necessary, OT saved by works+faith, Antichrist giant black alien in spaceship, believers transformed into 33-yo males, new Adams/Eves for alien planets).
panel: Who generally holds this view | Extreme KJV-only/hyper groups, Ruckman followers, Gene Kim/Bibleline Ministries, fringe online "grace" circles. Rejected by mainstream IFB/evangelicals.
panel: Strength | Attempts to emphasize Paul's ministry (severely distorted).
panel-warn: Issue | Multiple gospels accursed ([[Galatians 1:8-9]]). False distinctions from Abraham/Jesus/Apostles. Adds works/extreme divisions. Even further off rails: cultic/racial views (Christian Identity, Black Hebrew Israelites, etc.) contradict gospel to all nations ([[Matthew 28:19]]; [[Revelation 7:9]]).

=== Recap
--- Romans 9–11 [title]
overline: Recap
heading: Romans 9–11 • Paul's Unified Argument
sub: No Inserted Future National Program • Faith Alone • No Boasting • Glory for All Believers

--- Paul's relentless flow (1)
kicker: Chapters 1–11
- All have sinned and fall short ([[Romans 3:23]]).
- No flesh justified by works of the law ([[Romans 3:20]]).
- Righteousness by faith, apart from the law ([[Romans 3:21-22]], [[Romans 3:28]]).
- The true Jew is inward, circumcision of the heart ([[Romans 2:28-29]]).
- Not all Israel is Israel; children of promise are the seed ([[Romans 9:6-8]]).

--- Paul's relentless flow (2)
kicker: Chapters 1–11
- Israel sought righteousness by works and stumbled ([[Romans 9:30-33]]; [[Romans 10:3]]).
- Zeal without knowledge; not submitted to God's righteousness ([[Romans 10:1-4]]).
- God stretched out hands all day to disobedient people ([[Romans 10:21]]).
- Has God cast away His people? God forbid, remnant by grace exists now ([[Romans 11:1-5]]).
- The rest blinded/hardened ([[Romans 11:7-10]]).

--- Paul's relentless flow (3)
kicker: Chapters 1–11
- Branches broken off for unbelief; wild branches grafted in by faith ([[Romans 11:17-22]]).
- **IF** they continue not in unbelief, they **may** be grafted in ([[Romans 11:23]], conditional, individual).
- **IF** Gentiles do not continue in His goodness, they too will be cut off, no boasting ([[Romans 11:22]]).
- Blindness in part until fulness of the Gentiles ([[Romans 11:25]]).
- And so **all Israel shall be saved**, the full redeemed people of God by faith ([[Romans 11:26]]; [[Galatians 6:16]]).

--- No boasting
kicker: Not of Works, Tribe, or Law
- Abraham had whereof to glory, but **not before God** ([[Romans 4:2]]).
- By grace through faith, **not of works, lest any man should boast** ([[Ephesians 2:8-9]]).
- Not by tribe, not by flesh, not by law-keeping, only humble belief ([[Romans 3:27]]; [[Romans 4:16]]; [[Galatians 3:11]]).
- God’s grace alone, no quality or condition besides faith in Christ.

--- Jesus' warnings of cutting off
- Kingdom taken and given to a nation bringing forth fruit ([[Matthew 21:43]]).
- Vineyard let to other husbandmen ([[Matthew 21:41]]; [[Mark 12:9]]).
- Children of the kingdom cast into outer darkness ([[Matthew 8:12]]).
- House left desolate ([[Matthew 23:38]]).
- Fig tree cursed and withered, fruitless Israel ([[Mark 11:14]]; [[Mark 11:20-21]]).
- Axe at the root; unfruitful trees hewn down ([[Matthew 3:10]]; [[Luke 13:6-9]]).

--- The future promise
lead: The future promise: resurrection and glorification for **all who believe**, from every nation, kindred, tongue, and people ([[Revelation 7:9-17]]; [[1 Corinthians 15:51-54]]; [[Philippians 3:20-21]]).
p: One unified story: promise → fulfillment in Christ → one people by faith → new covenant → old done away.
take: No postponed kingdom or separate national program inserted, only mercy to **whosoever believes**, with no boasting in flesh, works, tribe, or law.
